.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt LIMITS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm limits
.Nd process resource limits
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each process has nine resource limits.
Each has an enforced soft value and a hard value that bounds the soft value.
.Xr prlimit64 2
maintains them;
.Fn getrlimit ,
.Fn setrlimit
and, on i686, the kernel call
.Xr ugetrlimit 2
use the same table.
Fork inherits both values and exec preserves them, except that an exec
that runs a program with IDs its caller lacks, a set-user-ID or
set-group-ID program, lowers a soft
.Dv RLIMIT_STACK
above 8 MiB to 8 MiB, as Linux does.
.Bl -tag -width RLIMIT_NOFILE
.It Dv RLIMIT_NPROC
The number of threads with the same real user ID across the machine, each
thread of a process counting, as on Linux.
Fork and clone return
.Er EAGAIN
when the soft limit is reached.
Real user ID 0 is exempt.
A setuid operation that changes the real user ID moves the thread to the
new user's count.
.It Dv RLIMIT_NOFILE
One greater than the highest descriptor number the process may be given,
in a descriptor table it shares with another process as in one of its own;
1024 soft and 524288 hard by default.
No process, root included, may set a hard value above
.Pa /proc/sys/fs/nr_open ,
1048576 by default; such a call returns
.Er EPERM .
Open returns
.Er EMFILE
when no descriptor below the soft limit is free, and
.Er ENFILE
to a caller other than root when the machine has
.Pa /proc/sys/fs/file-max
open files.
Dup2 returns
.Er EBADF
for a destination at or above the soft limit.
The descriptor table starts at sixteen entries and doubles as it fills.
A program that uses
.Xr select 2
past descriptor 1023 passes sets wider than
.Dv FD_SETSIZE .
.It Dv RLIMIT_AS
The bytes covered by the process's virtual memory regions.
Growth past the soft limit makes mmap return
.Er ENOMEM
and leaves the break unchanged when brk fails.
.It Dv RLIMIT_STACK
The bytes of the stack an exec gives a program, 8 MiB soft by default.
.It Dv RLIMIT_CORE
The bytes of a core file, 0 soft by default, which leaves none, as
.Xr core 5
describes.
.It Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK
The bytes of memory a process may lock, 8 MiB soft and hard by default,
as on Linux:
the pages of its regions locked by
.Xr mlock 2 ,
.Xr mlockall 2
or
.Dv MAP_LOCKED ,
and the pages a driver pins for a device on its behalf.
A caller with effective user ID 0 is exempt.
.Fn mlock
past the soft limit fails with
.Er ENOMEM ,
and with a soft limit of 0 with
.Er EPERM ;
.Dv MAP_LOCKED
and growth under
.Dv MCL_FUTURE
fail with
.Er EAGAIN .
.It Dv RLIMIT_FSIZE
The bytes a file the process writes may reach, no limit by default.
A write that would pass the soft limit writes the bytes below it, and one
that starts at the limit fails.
That write, a truncation past the limit and an
.Xr fallocate 2
that would grow the file past it fail with
.Er EFBIG
and send
.Dv SIGXFSZ
to the calling thread.
.It Dv RLIMIT_NICE
The lowest nice value a caller without privilege may give the process, as
20 less the value: 25 allows nice -5.
It is 0 soft and hard by default, as on Linux, which allows no nice value
below the present one.
.Xr setpriority 2
past it fails with
.Er EACCES ,
and
.Xr sched_setattr 2
and the
.Xr ia32 7
.Fn nice
with
.Er EPERM ;
leaving
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
needs it to allow the present nice value.
.It Dv RLIMIT_RTPRIO
The highest real-time priority a caller without privilege may give the
process, 0 soft and hard by default, as on Linux.
While it is 0,
.Xr sched_setscheduler 2
and
.Xr sched_setattr 2
refuse such a caller a real-time policy with
.Er EPERM ;
otherwise they refuse a priority above both it and the present one.
.El
.Ss Initial limits
Init reads
.Pa /etc/rach2/limits
at boot and sets its own limits before starting children.
Every session inherits these limits.
A missing file leaves the kernel defaults.
.Pp
Each line has a name,
.Ql nproc ,
.Ql nofile
or
.Ql as ,
followed by the soft and hard values.
Values are decimal numbers or
.Ql unlimited .
Address-space values are bytes.
The os-real image supplies:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nproc 256 512
nofile 1024 524288
as unlimited unlimited
.Ed
.Pp
The GNU root gives every user the kernel's own thread limit:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nproc 4096 4096
nofile 1024 524288
as unlimited unlimited
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Ic ulimit
built-in in
.Xr sh 1
reads and lowers limits.
.Ss Other limits
Process records are allocated from the kernel heap.
If the kernel cannot allocate a process record, fork returns
.Er ENOMEM .
Sockets and TCP control blocks also come from the heap, and so do a
descriptor table's growth and each open file description: a call that
makes a descriptor returns
.Er ENOMEM
when the heap has no room for them, and
.Er EMFILE
only at the descriptor limit.
These allocations are outside the resource-limit table.
Other fixed capacities are the loopback ring, 64 frames;
the USB device table, sixteen records; and the disk registry, eight records.
A user namespace may be nested 7 deep below the first, and so may a PID
namespace; its ID maps have at most 340 extents each, as on Linux.
Mount namespaces are bounded by memory and by
.Pa /proc/sys/user/max_mnt_namespaces ,
the namespaces each user may own at once, as
.Xr proc 5
describes it: half the threads Linux would allow the memory, 8192 on an
i686 machine of 1 GiB and 4096 on an x86-64 one, past which
.Xr unshare 2
fails with
.Er ENOSPC .
A KVM virtual machine,
.Xr kvm 4 ,
has at most 1024 vCPUs, with ids below 4096, and 256 memory slots;
a vCPU takes at most 256 CPUID entries, and fewer than 256 MSRs in one
call.
A VFIO container,
.Xr vfio 4 ,
holds at most 65535 DMA mappings, whose pages count against
.Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK ;
the machine has 64 IOMMU groups and 128 functions in groups at most, and
a function's MSI-X table at most 2048 entries.
The devices' messages share 191 interrupt vectors, from 48 to 239 less the
system call's 128; a request past them fails with
.Er ENOSPC .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/rach2/limits
.It Pa /etc/rach2/limits
Initial soft and hard limits read by init.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl cat /etc/rach2/limits
.Pp
The default test suite's
.Nm limitprobe
checks the refusals of the process, descriptor and address-space limits
by name.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2 ,
.Xr kvm 4 ,
.Xr vfio 4 ,
.Xr core 5 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr memory 7
